WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:06.691
We introduce our paper, "Same action, different outcome: variability in dynamic cloth manipulation".

00:07.350 --> 00:17.552
In most cloth simulators, identical trajectories applied to the cloth result in identical outcomes. In the real world, however, we get a distribution of outcomes.

00:18.052 --> 00:30.965
For example, in dynamic folding, ten replays on two very different fabrics. The thick wool lands in almost the same place for each rollout, while the light satin lands in distinctly different places on the table.

00:31.465 --> 00:36.083
Stacked on one photograph, the ten outlines show how much the cloth outcome varies.

00:36.583 --> 00:55.270
To measure this variability, we used a bimanual Franka setup. For each task and cloth, the robot replays a demonstrated trajectory ten times. Nineteen retroreflective markers track the cloth keypoints, and a stereo RGB-D camera records the image streams during the motions.

00:58.850 --> 01:07.580
All streams are synchronized on one wall clock, so at any instant the markers, the frames, the depth and the robot samples describe the same moment.

01:08.350 --> 01:21.454
This lets us reconstruct every episode in 3-D, here for the four dynamic tasks. Swing the cloth and place it flat. Fold it with one hand. Join two corners linearly. And swing to join them.

01:21.954 --> 01:26.470
We ran the tasks in various configurations, including the execution speed,

01:26.970 --> 01:31.992
as well as fabric types with extreme physical properties, wool, satin and chiffon.

01:32.492 --> 01:36.598
This results in twenty-seven configurations, with ten replays each.

01:37.850 --> 01:51.061
We measure how far apart the free edges of two replays settle, marker by marker, averaged over all pairs. That is our variability metric, D-bar. On a grasped corner it stays here under a tenth of a millimetre.

01:51.561 --> 02:00.199
Following the motion, the variability starts at its minimum, grows during the motion, and sometimes peaks before the motion ends and the cloth settles.

02:00.850 --> 02:10.897
The variability changes dramatically, from one millimetre up to more than a hundred. The cloth type is the dominant factor, and in most tasks, higher velocity increases it.

02:11.397 --> 02:23.910
We also tried a camera-only analysis, with SAM 2 for the mask, FoundationStereo for depth, and DINOv3 patches as virtual markers. Its segmentation is not always clean.

02:24.410 --> 02:30.161
The camera keeps the general trend of the variability, but it doesn't keep its values nor its ordering.

02:30.850 --> 02:34.465
And simulation? Every no-regrasp rollout, replayed.

02:35.350 --> 02:45.124
Calibrated per fabric, driven by the measured inputs, the simulated cloth follows the real motion. Replayed ten times, it does not scatter like the real one.

02:45.850 --> 02:53.304
No engine reproduces the real variability, in magnitude or in order. Clothilde comes closest, then Genesis.

02:53.850 --> 02:58.522
Everything here, the recordings and the simulator twins, is on the project page.
